   "thinking": "Now I have a clear picture. The problem is:\n\n1. `ledger-queue-0` is stuck in `Pending` because its PersistentVolumeClaim (`data-ledger-queue-0`) is bound to a PersistentVolume (`local-pv-ip-10-0-7-21-nvme1`) that has node affinity pinned to `ip-10-0-7-21.ec2.internal` \u2014 the failed node.\n2. The storage class is `local-nvme` with a `local` volume type, which means the data physically lives on that specific node's NVMe drive.\n3. The scheduler error confirms this \u2014 all eight healthy nodes have a volume node affinity conflict since the PV only permits scheduling on the dead node. Local PVs are inherently node-specific, so when that node goes down, the data becomes unreachable, and since the reclaim policy is `Delete`, removing the PVC/PV would wipe out the volume record even though the underlying disk data might still physically exist on the node.\n\nThis is genuinely a data-loss scenario: Redis is running with append-only persistence on a local NVMe drive bound to the failed node `ip-10-0-7-21`. If infra can recover that node, `ledger-queue-0` reschedules automatically once it's Ready again; if not, the AOF data is almost certainly gone, and recreating the PVC/PV means starting Redis fresh and losing any buffered settlement messages.\n\nGiven the node affinity lock on the PV and the `local-nvme` storage class, there's no safe migration path without data loss \u2014 any attempt to create a new PV on a different node and repoint the PVC would abandon the old AOF entirely. This decision point around data loss and storage class choices is beyond what I should resolve unilaterally, so I need to escalate it clearly, laying out the recovery-wait option versus the lossy-migration option.\n\nThe real concern is the append-only Redis data buffering settlement messages on that local NVMe volume -- losing it means lost settlements. Given the data loss risk, the need to either wait on node recovery or make a re-provisioning decision with real tradeoffs,